    "hag": {
      "word": "HAG",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "A witch, sorceress, or enchantress; also, a wizard. [Obs.] \"[Silenus] that old hag.\" Golding.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "An ugly old woman.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A fury; a she-monster. Grashaw.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Zoöl.)",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "An eel-like marine marsipobranch (Myxine glutinosa), allied to the lamprey. It has a suctorial mouth, with labial appendages, and a single pair of gill openings. It is the type of the order Hyperotpeta. Called also hagfish, borer, slime eel, sucker, and sleepmarken.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Zoöl.)",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "The hagdon or shearwater.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "An appearance of light and fire on a horse's mane or a man's hair. Blount. Hag moth (Zoöl.), a moth (Phobetron pithecium), the larva of which has curious side appendages, and feeds on fruit trees. matting or pointing.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "To harass; to weary with vexation. How are superstitious men hagged out of their wits with the fancy of omens. L'Estrange.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "A small wood, or part of a wood or copse, which is marked off or inclosed for felling, or which has been felled. This said, he led me over hoults and hags; Through thorns and bushes scant my legs I drew. Fairfax.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A quagmire; mossy ground where peat or turf has been cut. Dugdale.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        }
      ],
